WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.660
Why does the first division of meiosis part homologs, and not sisters?

2
00:00:09.100 --> 00:00:12.260
In mitosis, homologs do not synapse as a pair.

3
00:00:13.560 --> 00:00:16.680
Each chromosome lines up on its own, and its sisters part.

4
00:00:17.750 --> 00:00:21.890
In meiosis I, the sisters stay together, and the homologs part.

5
00:00:23.170 --> 00:00:26.270
So something must tie each pair, and let go in the right order.

6
00:00:27.333 --> 00:00:28.553
Start with the sisters.

7
00:00:29.993 --> 00:00:34.133
From S phase on, ring-shaped proteins called cohesin tie them together.

8
00:00:35.473 --> 00:00:38.573
The rings sit along the arms and around the centromere.

9
00:00:39.583 --> 00:00:43.023
In prophase I, the homologs pair along their length.

10
00:00:44.163 --> 00:00:48.803
A protein structure called the synaptonemal complex zips them together.

11
00:00:49.933 --> 00:00:52.353
While they are zipped, crossing over happens.

12
00:00:53.753 --> 00:00:58.233
Segments are exchanged between non-sister chromatids, one from each homolog.

13
00:00:59.553 --> 00:01:03.193
It starts with a deliberate break in the DNA of one chromatid.

14
00:01:04.393 --> 00:01:07.933
The repair copies from the other homolog, and can end in a swap.

15
00:01:09.283 --> 00:01:12.423
Later in prophase I, the zipper comes apart.

16
00:01:13.683 --> 00:01:16.163
The homologs pull slightly away from each other.

17
00:01:17.203 --> 00:01:19.523
But they stay joined where the crossover was.

18
00:01:20.703 --> 00:01:22.663
That visible site is a chiasma.

19
00:01:23.667 --> 00:01:25.367
Now look at what ties the pair.

20
00:01:26.587 --> 00:01:31.467
The crossover, plus the cohesin on the arms beyond it, holds the two homologs

21
00:01:31.467 --> 00:01:37.167
together. In most organisms studied, that is the link the spindle pulls against.

22
00:01:38.850 --> 00:01:42.990
So what must the cell cut, to let the homologs go at anaphase I?

23
00:01:49.483 --> 00:01:51.183
The cohesin on the arms.

24
00:01:52.503 --> 00:01:55.443
Once it is cut, the crossover no longer ties the pair.

25
00:01:56.663 --> 00:01:59.223
The homologs part, each still made of two sisters.

26
00:02:00.467 --> 00:02:03.167
Cohesin at the centromeres has to survive.

27
00:02:04.507 --> 00:02:08.167
A protein called shugoshin protects it until anaphase II.

28
00:02:09.447 --> 00:02:13.287
Shugoshin brings an enzyme, PP2A, to the centromeres.

29
00:02:14.547 --> 00:02:18.207
Removing phosphate there is thought to shield cohesin from being cut.

30
00:02:19.250 --> 00:02:21.910
So the sisters stay joined into meiosis II.

31
00:02:23.190 --> 00:02:27.910
At anaphase II, the centromere cohesin is cut as well, and the sisters part.

32
00:02:29.483 --> 00:02:34.203
This two-step release is the pattern seen in yeast and mammals, as studied.

33
00:02:35.483 --> 00:02:37.183
One cut frees the homologs.

34
00:02:38.163 --> 00:02:39.763
A later cut frees the sisters.

35
00:02:40.967 --> 00:02:42.907
Each link is a point of failure.

36
00:02:44.227 --> 00:02:49.707
In mouse eggs, if centromere cohesin is lost too early, sisters reach meiosis II

37
00:02:49.707 --> 00:02:53.807
already apart. They can then go to either pole at random.

38
00:02:55.147 --> 00:02:57.567
Eggs can end up with too many or too few chromosomes.

39
00:02:58.817 --> 00:03:02.777
As studied in yeast and mammals, two links are cut in two steps.

40
00:03:04.137 --> 00:03:07.497
Arm cohesin goes at anaphase I, and the homologs part.

41
00:03:08.917 --> 00:03:12.417
Centromere cohesin goes at anaphase II, and the sisters part.
